---
// How the atlas is made: what is checked by machine, what is a judgement, and how to disagree.
import Read from '../../layouts/Read.astro';
import Coffee from '../../components/Coffee.astro';
import { cards, questions, thinkers, links, REGIONS } from '../../lib/atlas.js';
const cover = REGIONS.map(([id, label]) => [label, cards.filter(c => c.region === id).length]);
const all = cards.flatMap(c => Object.values(c.readings));
const n = s => all.filter(r => r.second === s).length;
const unscored = all.filter(r => r.score === 'unscored').length;
const sample = questions[0];
---
<Read title="How Svapna works: what is checked, what is a judgement — Svapna" description={`How the ${cards.length} passages in the atlas are found, checked word for word against their sources, placed on a scale, read a second time, and corrected.`} path="/how-it-works" crumbs={[{ name: 'How it works', path: '/how-it-works' }]}>
  <p class="small crumb"><a href="/">Svapna</a> · How it works</p>
  <h1>What is checked, <em>and what is a judgement.</em></h1>
  <p class="lede">The atlas holds {cards.length} short passages from {thinkers.length} thinkers and texts, placed {all.length} times across {questions.length} questions. The quotes are verified. The placements are readings, and this page says exactly how far to trust them.</p>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>1. The words are checked by machine</h2>
    <p>Every passage is a short run of words copied exactly from a text that can be read online: a public-domain edition, the author’s or publisher’s own page, an institution, or a line quoted in a scholarly encyclopedia and labelled as such. Each passage links to that page.</p>
    <p>Before a passage is published, a program fetches the linked page and confirms the excerpt is there word for word. A passage that fails is not published. Where the original is not in English, the original is quoted and checked, and the English beside it is the atlas’s own unreviewed translation, marked as such.</p>
    <p>Unauthorised copies of books still in copyright are not used as sources.</p>

    <h2>2. The placement is a reading</h2>
    <p>Each question has a scale of three positions, with a one-line definition for each, shown on the question’s page. For “{sample.plain}” they are: {sample.steps.filter(s => s.tone !== 'none').map(s => s.plain.toLowerCase()).join(', ')}.</p>
    <p>A passage is placed where its own words put it. The rule is strict: the excerpt itself must support the position. What the author is known for, or what the surrounding chapter argues, is not enough. Where the words do not settle the question, the passage is marked <em>unscored</em>, which is not a low score or a disagreement. {unscored} of {all.length} placements are unscored.</p>
    <p>Each passage also carries a note on what it does not show: the genre, the translation, the speaker (a character in a play is not the playwright), and what the author says elsewhere.</p>

    <p>Scriptures are quoted the same way as any other text, from a named translation, and placed by their own words. That is a statement about one passage, not a judgement of a faith, and a tradition’s scripture often sits at more than one position. The Bible is quoted from the King James Version because it is in the public domain and can be checked word for word; where another translation is used, the passage names it. The counts on each question’s page describe what has been gathered so far. They are not a vote, and nothing is ranked.</p>

    <h2>3. Who does the reading</h2>
    <p>The passages were gathered and first placed by AI research agents working to a written brief, under the direction of Flint, the independent researcher who runs the site. No human editor has yet reviewed the placements one by one, and no page on the site claims otherwise.</p>

    <h2>4. Every placement was read twice</h2>
    <p>A second reader, also an AI agent, was given only the question, the scale and the quoted words, without the first placement, and placed every passage again. Where the two disagreed, a third reading saw both and decided by the rule above. The outcome is printed under each passage:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>{n('agreed')}</strong> placements: both readers placed it in the same position.</li>
      <li><strong>{n('settled')}</strong>: the readers differed and the third reading settled it.</li>
      <li><strong>{n('differed')}</strong>: the readers differed and the passage could fairly sit either way. Treat these as open.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Where the second reading moved a placement, the passage says so, with the reason. Both lists are public: <a href="/how-it-works/open">every placement still open</a>, and <a href="/how-it-works/changes">every placement that was changed, and why</a>.</p>

    <h2>5. What a placement does not claim</h2>
    <p>A placement describes a few quoted lines. It is not a verdict on a thinker’s whole work, and the same thinker can sit at different positions in different passages. Three positions flatten real differences; the scale is a way to find people to read, not a classification of philosophies.</p>
    <p>Matches, circles and stickers are computed from these placements and a visitor’s own answers. They are an invitation to read further.</p>

    <h2>6. Places, dates and connections</h2>
    <p>Each passage is pinned where it was written, taught or delivered. Some pins cite a page that states the place; others are a traditional association or still await confirmation, and each passage’s page says which. Dates of ancient texts are approximate and marked “c.”.</p>
    <p>The {links.length} connections between thinkers are documented ones only: one thinker citing, answering or writing to another, with the caveat that applies. Similar ideas are not shown as influence.</p>

    <p>The one-line description under each thinker’s name, and the tradition each is filed under, were written by machine for orientation. They are not sources; the passages are. A voice is filed under one tradition for browsing, which is cruder than the truth for many of them.</p>

    <h2 id="coverage">7. Where the atlas is thin</h2>
    <p>The atlas is not evenly spread, and it should not be read as if it were. By where each passage is pinned: {cover.map(([label, n], i) => <>{i > 0 && ', '}{label} {n}</>)}. Europe is over-represented because far more of its texts are freely readable online in a form a machine can check. Sub-Saharan Africa, Latin America and Oceania are thin, and many voices there reach the atlas only through a collector’s or missionary’s report, which each passage says. Filling these is ongoing work, and suggestions of checkable sources are welcome.</p>

    <h2>8. Disagree with a placement</h2>
    <p>Every passage has a “Disagree?” link. It opens a short form with the passage already named. Say where the passage should sit and why, ideally pointing at the words. Corrections to quotes, dates, places and sources go the same way. Voting on placements by members is planned; until then every message is read by a person.</p>
    <p><a href="/about/contact">Write to Svapna →</a></p>
    <p id="credits"><strong>Corrections from readers.</strong> When a reader’s correction is used, the passage is changed and the reader is named here, if they gave a name. None has been received yet; the first will be listed here.</p>

    <h2>9. Check it yourself</h2>
    <p>The whole atlas can be downloaded: every passage, its source and locator, every placement with its reason, and the outcome of the second reading. <a href="/data">The data page</a> explains every column. <a href="/data/atlas.json">As JSON</a> · <a href="/data/atlas.csv">as a spreadsheet (CSV)</a>. The placements and notes are offered under CC BY 4.0; the quoted passages belong to their sources.</p>
    <p><strong>Addresses are kept.</strong> A thinker’s page and each passage’s anchor do not change; where a name has been corrected or two spellings merged, the old address forwards to the new one. Every passage shows the date its quote was last checked against the source. Sources are mostly public-domain editions held by Project Gutenberg, the Internet Archive and Wikisource; a source that disappears is replaced or the passage is withdrawn, and the change is recorded.</p>
    <p>Each passage has a fixed address that can be cited: a thinker’s page, followed by the passage’s own anchor. When citing a passage, cite the source it links to as well; Svapna is the finding aid, not the edition.</p>
    <h2 id="teachers">10. For teachers</h2>
    <p>Nothing on the site needs an account, and no student data is collected: answers stay in each student’s own browser. A lesson that works: project a question from the home page and have the class vote; open that question’s page, narrow it by era or region, and use “Copy a link to this set” or “Print it” to hand out five or six contrasting passages with their sources; then ask where each passage should sit on the scale, and compare with the placement and its “read twice” line. The passages are primary sources quoted briefly; the placements are the part to argue with.</p>
  </div>
  <Coffee />
</Read>
